{"text": "C.R. Palevol 15 (2016) 317-339\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect\n\nComptes Rendus Palevol\n\nwww.sciencedirect.com\n\nHuman Palaeontology and Prehistory\n\nIntentional cut marks on bovid from the Quranwala zone, \u00ae CrossMark\n2.6 Ma, Siwalik Frontal Range, northwestern India\n\nTraces de boucheries intentionnelles sur des bovid\u00e9s de la zone\nQuranwala, 2,6 Ma, chaine frontale de Siwaliks, Inde du Nord-Ouest\n\nAnne Dambricourt Malass\u00e9*, Anne-Marie Moigne**, Mukesh Singh\u201d,\nThomas Calligaro\u2018, Baldev Karir?, Claire Gaillard?, Amandeep Kaur\u201d,\nVipnesh Bhardwaj\u201d, Surinder Pal>, Salah Abdessadok\u00ae, C\u00e9cile Chapon Sao?,\nJulien Gargani\u201c, Alina Tudryn\u201c, Miguel Garcia Sanz\u00b0\n\n@ Histoire naturelle de I'Homme pr\u00e9historique (HNHP, UMR 7194 CNRS), Institut de pal\u00e9ontologie humaine, 1, rue Ren\u00e9-Panhard,\n75013 Paris, France\n> Society for Archaeological and Anthropological Research, Chandigarh, India\n\u00a9 Centre de recherche et de restauration des mus\u00e9es de France (C2RMF), palais du Louvre, pavillon de Flore, 75001 Paris, France\n4 G\u00e9osciences Paris-Sud (GEOPS, UMR 8148 CNRS), universit\u00e9 Paris-Sud-11, Orsay, France\n\u00a9 Plateforme AST-RX (OMSI, UMS 2700), Paris, France\n\nARTICLE INFO ABSTRACT\nArticle history: The Indo-French research program \u2018Siwaliks\u2019 has been surveying the Late Pliocene Forma-\nReceived 23 June 2015 tion of the Chandigarh anticline (NW India) since 2008. These sub-Himalayan floodplain\nAccepted after revision 17 September 2015 deposits are known for their Tertiary-Quaternary transitional fauna, especially those from\n\nAvailable online 28 January 2016 the Quranwala zone in the Masol Formation, whose basal member is approximately 130\n\nmeters below the Gauss/Matuyama paleomagnetic reversal (2.588 Ma). About 1500 fossils\nhave been collected in the inlier of Masol, most often on recently eroded outcrops, and\nsometimes in association with stone tools (choppers, flakes). Many bones were covered by\na variety of marks (animal, bioerosion and tectonics) and among these traces a few were\nintentional cut marks. Different methods have been applied in Paris (France) to describe\ntheir topography on a micron scale, using the 3D Digital Video Microscope Hirox, and com-\n\nHandled by Anne Dambricourt Malass\u00e9\n\nKeywords:\nSiwaliks\nSub-Himalayan floodplain\nPlio/Pleistocene transitional fauna\n\nCut marks pleted with binocular microscopy at the Center for Research and Restoration of Museums\nQuartzite cobbles of France (C2RMF), and X-ray microtomography with the AST-RX platform, at the National\nExperimental protocol Museum of Natural History, Paris. Experiments with quartzite cobbles collected near the\n\nfossils were carried out in India and in France. The mineralization of the traces is identi-\ncal to the bone tissue, and comparison with our experimental cut marks confirms that the\nprofiles are typical of the sharp edge of a flake or cobble in quartzite; their size and spatial\norganization testify to energetic and intentional gestures from an agile wrist acting with\nprecision, and to a good knowledge of the bovid anatomy.\n\u00a9 2015 Acad\u00e9mie des sciences. Published by Elsevier Masson SAS. This is an open access\narticle under the CC-BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/\nby-nc-nd/4.0/).\n\n* Corresponding author at: UMR 7194 CNRS/MNHN/EPCC, avenue L\u00e9on-Jean-Gr\u00e9gory, 66720 Tautavel, France.\nE-mail address: moigne@mnhn.fr (A.-M. Moigne).\n\nhttp://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.crpv.2015.09.019\n1631-0683/\u00a9 2015 Acad\u00e9mie des sciences. Published by Elsevier Masson SAS. This is an open access article under the CC-BY-NC-ND license\n(http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).",
